    "solution": "Two unknown supporting forces, so we choose **the pivot at A** so that $F_\\mathrm{A}$ gets the lever arm 0 and disappears from the principle of moments. What remains is the moment of the gravitational force (clockwise) against the moment of $F_\\mathrm{B}$ (anticlockwise):\n\n$$ F_\\mathrm{B} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{B} = F_\\mathrm{G} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{G} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad F_\\mathrm{B} = \\frac{F_\\mathrm{G} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{G}}{l_\\mathrm{B}} $$\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nF_\\mathrm{G} = m \\cdot g = 30\\ \\mathrm{kg} \\cdot 9.82\\ \\mathrm{N/kg} = 294.6\\ \\mathrm{N} \\\\\nl_\\mathrm{G} = 2.0\\ \\mathrm{m}\\ \\text{(A to the centre of gravity in the middle of the plank)} \\\\\nl_\\mathrm{B} = 4.0 - 1.0 = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{m}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ F_\\mathrm{B} = \\frac{294.6 \\cdot 2.0}{3.0} = 196.4\\ \\mathrm{N} \\approx 0.20\\ \\mathrm{kN} $$\n\nThe lengths and the mass have two significant figures, so the answer is rounded to two significant figures.\n\n**Answer:** About $0.20\\ \\mathrm{kN}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** It is a good idea to check with force equilibrium: $F_\\mathrm{A} = 294.6 - 196.4 \\approx 98\\ \\mathrm{N}$. B is closer to the centre of gravity than A and, as expected, carries the larger share of the weight."
