    "solution": "Two unknown supporting forces. We choose **the pivot at the right support R** so that $F_\\mathrm{R}$ gets the lever arm 0 and disappears. What remains is rotational equilibrium about R: the weights of the person and the board turn anticlockwise, and the left supporting force turns clockwise. The lever arms are measured from R: the person stands $4.0 - 1.0 = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from R, the board's centre of gravity (in the middle of the board) $4.0 - 2.0 = 2.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from R and the left trestle $4.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from R.\n\n$$ F_\\mathrm{person} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{person} + F_\\mathrm{G} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{G} = F_\\mathrm{L} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{L} \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad F_\\mathrm{L} = \\frac{F_\\mathrm{person} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{person} + F_\\mathrm{G} \\cdot l_\\mathrm{G}}{l_\\mathrm{L}} $$\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nF_\\mathrm{person} = m_\\mathrm{person} \\cdot g = 60\\ \\mathrm{kg} \\cdot 9.82\\ \\mathrm{N/kg} = 589.2\\ \\mathrm{N} \\\\\nF_\\mathrm{G} = m_\\text{board} \\cdot g = 15\\ \\mathrm{kg} \\cdot 9.82\\ \\mathrm{N/kg} = 147.3\\ \\mathrm{N} \\\\\nl_\\mathrm{person} = 3.0\\ \\mathrm{m} \\\\\nl_\\mathrm{G} = 2.0\\ \\mathrm{m} \\\\\nl_\\mathrm{L} = 4.0\\ \\mathrm{m}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ F_\\mathrm{L} = \\frac{589.2 \\cdot 3.0 + 147.3 \\cdot 2.0}{4.0} = \\frac{2\\,062.2}{4.0} = 515.55\\ \\mathrm{N} \\approx 520\\ \\mathrm{N} $$\n\nAll given values have two significant figures, so the answer is rounded to two significant figures.\n\n**Answer:** About $520\\ \\mathrm{N}$.\n\n**General conclusion:** With several gravitational forces on the same board, each force gets its own moment with its own lever arm: the principle of moments becomes a sum on each side. It is a good idea to check with force equilibrium: $F_\\mathrm{R} = (589.2 + 147.3) - 515.55 \\approx 220\\ \\mathrm{N}$, and as expected the left trestle carries the most because the person stands closest to it."
