    "solution": "The box topples when **the centre of gravity ends up outside the pivot**, that is when the vertical line of action of the gravitational force through the centre of gravity passes the tipping edge. At the limit the centre of gravity is directly above the edge, and for a uniform box with width $b$ and height $h$ the toppling angle $\\alpha$ satisfies\n\n$$ \\tan \\alpha = \\frac{b}{h} $$\n\n$$\n\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\nb = 0.60\\ \\mathrm{m} \\\\\nh = 0.80\\ \\mathrm{m}\n\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n$$ \\tan \\alpha = \\frac{0.60}{0.80} = 0.75 $$\n\n$$ \\alpha = \\tan^{-1}(0.75) = 36.869...^\\circ \\approx 37^\\circ $$\n\n**Answer:** The box topples if it is tilted more than about $37^\\circ$.\n\n**General conclusion:** This is the formula for the toppling angle, $\\tan \\alpha = \\dfrac{b}{h}$: a wide, low box can be tilted a lot before it topples. As long as the plumb line through the centre of gravity meets the base of support, the object stands stably. If it ends up outside, the object falls."
